# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE PRODUCTION BUILD — what must NOT be in it
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Vite loads `.env` for every build and `.env.production` on top of it
# for `vite build`. So this file exists to take things AWAY.
#
# ⚠ VITE_ALLOW_DEV_AUTH=1 SHIPPED TO THE LIVE SITE. The deployed sign-in
# page carried a "DEVELOPMENT ONLY — Owner · every property · group
# admin" panel, on the real domain, for anybody who opened it.
#
# It was not a way in: the API refuses to start with dev auth in
# production, and the preflight checks that. Pressing it earns a 401.
# But a shortcut labelled "group admin" on a hotel's live login screen
# is alarming to anybody who sees it, and the day somebody sets that
# variable on the server to debug something, it stops being cosmetic.
#
# Found by looking at the deployed page, not by any build check —
# `npm run build` succeeded, all three bundles were clean, and nothing
# anywhere said the development shortcut was in them.
VITE_ALLOW_DEV_AUTH=

# The API address is NOT set here. It is written at build time into
# dist/config.json by `npm run build:app`, and resolved at RUNTIME, so
# the backend can move without rebuilding every client.
